<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guy Fawkes Night</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guy Fawkes Day, Bonfire Night, Fireworks Night, Gunpowder Treason Day</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guy Fawkes Night is an annual commemoration observed on 5 November, primarily in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, with bonfires and fireworks displays. It began in 1605, when Guy Fawkes, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder-plot">Gunpowder Plot</a>, was arrested while guarding explosives placed beneath the House of Lords, and people lit bonfires around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to celebrate the survival of King James I. The Observance of 5th November Act 1605 mandated an annual public day of thanksgiving, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> Treason Day it became a focus for anti-Catholic sentiment, with effigies of the Pope burnt. Towards the end of the 18th century children began begging for money with effigies of Guy Fawkes. The Act was repealed in 1859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guy-fawkes-night.jpg" alt="Guy Fawkes Night" width="330" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Sandby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Windsor_castle_guyfawkesnight1776.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shortly after midnight, Guy Fawkes was caught guarding gunpowder in a cellar under the House of Lords, foiling a Catholic plot to kill James I. Bonfires were lit in celebration, beginning the tradition of <b>Bonfire Night</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-5">5 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
